      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Specific immune response to allergens is decisive in the development of clinically healthy or allergic states."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "Recent developments in the mechanisms of allergen-specific peripheral tolerance can be used for future treatment modalities."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "This review focuses on recent developments in allergen tolerance."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "RESULTS/CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The balance between allergen-specific IL-10-secreting T regulatory cells (Tr1) and T helper 2 (Th2) cells appears to be decisive in the development of allergic and healthy immune response against allergens. Induction of IL-10- and TGF-beta-producing Tr1 cells, IgG4-isotype-blocking antibodies, and suppressed mast cells, basophils and eosinophils represent major components of a relatively normalized immune response after allergen-specific immunotherapy and healthy immune response to aeroallergens in sensitized individuals."
        }
      ]},
